1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,980
KATIE: Joe, you know I'm giving a presentation in our film studies class next week?

2
00:00:07,980 --> 00:00:08,910
JOE: Yes.

3
00:00:08,910 --> 00:00:14,900
KATIE: Well, could we discuss it? I could do with getting someone else's opinion.

4
00:00:14,900 --> 00:00:18,790
JOE: Of course. Katie. What are you going to talk about?

5
00:00:18,790 --> 00:00:23,007
KATIE: It's about film adaptations of Shakespeare's plays.

6
00:00:23,007 --> 00:00:29,860
I've got very interested in all the different approaches that film directors take.

7
00:00:29,860 --> 00:00:31,210
JOE: Uhuh.

8
00:00:31,210 --> 00:00:34,935
KATIE: So I thought I'd start with Giannetti,

9
00:00:34,935 --> 00:00:42,384
who's a professor of film and literature, and in one of his books he came up

10
00:00:42,384 --> 00:00:51,230
with a straightforward classification of film adaptations based on how faithful they are to the original plays and novels.

11
00:00:51,230 --> 00:00:52,810
JOE: Right.

12
00:00:52,810 --> 00:00:55,800
KATIE: I've already made some notes on that

13
00:00:56,100 --> 00:01:00,910
so I just need to sort those out before the presentation.

14
00:01:00,910 --> 00:01:06,061
I thought that next I'd ask the class to come up

15
00:01:06,061 --> 00:01:12,617
with the worst examples of Shakespeare adaptations that they've seen, and to say why.

16
00:01:12,617 --> 00:01:17,300
That should be more fun than having their favourite versions.

17
00:01:17,300 --> 00:01:21,500
JOE: Yes, I can certainly think of a couple!

18
00:01:21,500 --> 00:01:26,447
KATIE: Right. Next I want to talk about Rachel Malchow.

19
00:01:26,447 --> 00:01:32,879
I came across something on the internet about her work on film adaptations,

20
00:01:32,879 --> 00:01:39,310
and I was thinking of showing some film clips to illustrate her ideas.

21
00:01:39,310 --> 00:01:45,510
JOE: Will you have enough time, though? Both to prepare and during the presentation?

22
00:01:45,510 --> 00:01:50,530
After all, I doubt if you'll be able to find all the clips you want.

23
00:01:50,530 --> 00:01:58,245
KATIE: Hmm. Perhaps you're right. OK, well, I'd better do some slides instead,

24
00:01:58,245 --> 00:02:05,960
saying how various films relate to what she says. That should encourage discussion.

25
00:02:05,960 --> 00:02:07,680
JOE: Mmm.

26
00:02:07,680 --> 00:02:14,676
KATIE: Next I want to say something about how plays may be chosen for adaptation

27
00:02:14,676 --> 00:02:20,740
because they're concerned with issues of the time when the film is made.

28
00:02:20,740 --> 00:02:25,480
JOE: You mean things like patriotism, or the role of governments?

29
00:02:25,480 --> 00:02:33,210
KATIE: Exactly. It's quite tricky, but I've got a few ideas I'd like to discuss.

30
00:02:33,210 --> 00:02:42,134
KATIE: And finally I want to talk about a few adaptations that I think illustrate a range of approaches,

31
00:02:42,134 --> 00:02:48,240
and make some comments on them. Do you know the Japanese film Ran?

32
00:02:48,240 --> 00:02:54,390
JOE: I haven't seen it. It was based on Shakespeare's King Lear, wasn't it?

33
00:02:54,390 --> 00:02:58,900
KATIE: That's right. It was a very loose adaptation

34
00:02:59,340 --> 00:03:02,590
using the same situation and story,

35
00:03:02,590 --> 00:03:09,150
but moving it to 16th century Japan instead of 16th century Britain.

36
00:03:09,150 --> 00:03:13,523
So for example the king's daughters become sons,

37
00:03:13,523 --> 00:03:20,630
because in Japanese culture at that time, women couldn't succeed to the throne.

38
00:03:20,630 --> 00:03:27,430
JOE: OK. I hope you're going to talk about the 1993 film of Much Ado About Nothing.

39
00:03:27,430 --> 00:03:31,080
I think that's one of the best Shakespeare films

40
00:03:31,540 --> 00:03:34,630
It really brings the play to life, doesn't it?

41
00:03:34,630 --> 00:03:38,757
KATIE: Yes, I agree. And I think filming it in Italy,

42
00:03:38,757 --> 00:03:45,510
where the play is set, makes you see what life was like at the time of the play.

43
00:03:45,510 --> 00:03:49,510
JOE: Absolutely. Right, what's next?

44
00:03:49,510 --> 00:03:55,760
KATIE: Er, next, I thought Romeo & Juliet, the 1996 film

45
00:03:56,020 --> 00:03:59,060
which moves the action into the present day.

46
00:03:59,060 --> 00:04:01,852
JOE: Yes, it worked really well,

47
00:04:01,852 --> 00:04:08,832
I thought - changing the two feuding families in the original to two competing business empires,

48
00:04:08,832 --> 00:04:13,950
even though they're speaking in the English of the original play.

49
00:04:13,950 --> 00:04:17,500
KATIE: You'd expect it would sound really bizarre

50
00:04:17,680 --> 00:04:21,170
but I found I soon got used to it.

51
00:04:21,170 --> 00:04:23,030
JOE: Me too.

52
00:04:23,030 --> 00:04:28,075
KATIE: Then I thought I'd include a real Hollywood film,

53
00:04:28,075 --> 00:04:33,120
one that's intended to appeal to a mass commercial audience.

54
00:04:33,120 --> 00:04:36,170
JOE: There must be quite a number of those.

55
00:04:36,170 --> 00:04:41,455
KATIE: Yes, but I've picked the 1996 film of Hamlet.

56
00:04:41,455 --> 00:04:49,910
It included every line of the text, but it's more like a typical action hero movie -

57
00:04:49,910 --> 00:04:56,780
there are loads of special effects, but no unifying interpretation of the play.

58
00:04:56,780 --> 00:04:59,870
JOE: All show and no substance.

59
00:04:59,870 --> 00:05:05,550
KATIE: Exactly. Then there's Prospero's Books, based on The Tempest.

60
00:05:05,550 --> 00:05:11,230
That was really innovative, from a stylistic point of view.

61
00:05:11,230 --> 00:05:17,460
JOE: Didn't it include dance and singing and animation, as well as live actors?

62
00:05:17,460 --> 00:05:25,060
KATIE: Yes, it did. I also want to mention Looking for Richard. Did you ever see it?

63
00:05:25,060 --> 00:05:27,763
JOE: No. but I've read about it.

64
00:05:27,763 --> 00:05:33,940
It was a blend of a documentary with a few scenes from Richard lll, wasn't it?

65
00:05:33,940 --> 00:05:41,170
KATIE: That's right. It's more a way of looking into how people nowadays connect with the playwright -

66
00:05:41,170 --> 00:05:47,550
the play is really just the starting point. And that' ll be where I finish.

67
00:05:47,550 --> 00:05:52,010
JOE: Well, it sounds as though it'll be very interesting.
